---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
	title="Kilnbook vs Mindbody for pottery studios"
	description="Mindbody was built for gyms and spas. Kilnbook was built for pottery studios: kiln firing queues, firing fees by cubic inch, pickup texts, wheel reservations and self-filling waitlists."
>
	<section class="page-hero">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">Kilnbook vs Mindbody</p>
			<h1>Your studio is not a gym.</h1>
			<p class="lede narrow">
				Mindbody was built for gyms, yoga studios and spas. A pottery studio runs on kiln loads,
				firing fees, shared wheels and pieces waiting on the pickup shelf, and generic booking
				software has no place to put any of that.
			</p>
			<div class="hero-actions">
				<a href="/contact" class="btn">Book a demo</a>
				<a href="/pricing" class="btn btn-ghost">See pricing</a>
			</div>
			<p class="muted small">30-day free trial. No card needed to start.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-alt">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow">Kiln scheduling</p>
				<h2>Firing queues, not just time slots</h2>
				<p>
					Kilnbook manages a queue for every kiln, split into bisque and glaze loads, and works out
					each piece's firing fee by cubic inch as it joins the queue.
				</p>
				<p>
					Mindbody books people into time slots. It has no idea what a kiln load is, which firing
					stage a piece is at, or that clay moves from the shelf to the kiln to the pickup shelf.
				</p>
				<p><a href="/features/firings">How firing queues work</a></p>
			</div>
			<img
				src="/images/firing-queue.svg"
				alt="A shelf queue for one kiln showing a bisque load at 70 percent and a cone 6 glaze load ready to fire"
				width="480"
				height="320"
			/>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap section narrow">
		<h2>Firing fees that collect themselves</h2>
		<p>
			Kilnbook measures firing fees by cubic inch, then calculates and collects them automatically,
			so nobody spends a weekend reconciling the kiln log with a payment app. In Mindbody, firing
			fees end up as a manual workaround or a spreadsheet you reconcile separately.
			<a href="/features/firings">Firing fees in Kilnbook</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>"Is my bowl out yet?"</h2>
		<p>
			Members can check where their work is in the firing queue from their phone. When a load is
			unloaded, everyone with work in it gets a pickup text automatically. Mindbody has nothing that
			tracks work in progress, so those questions still come to the front desk.
			<a href="/features/firings">Pickup texts</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Memberships that know about wheels</h2>
		<p>
			In Kilnbook, wheel time and hand-building table reservations are tied directly to membership
			tier, so each member can book the equipment their membership includes. Mindbody's reservations are not aware of equipment types or the rules for who can use
			what on the studio floor.
			<a href="/features/wheel-time">Memberships and reservations</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Classes and packs built for ceramics</h2>
		<p>
			Sell six-week courses, drop-in sessions and class packs from one schedule. When a class is
			full, students join the waitlist, and if someone cancels the next person is moved into the
			open seat automatically, without anyone on staff lifting a finger. With Mindbody's generic class model, staff
			end up managing the waitlist by hand.
			<a href="/features/classes">Classes, packs and waitlists</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Software that speaks studio</h2>
		<p>
			Kilnbook knows what a bisque load is, and that members constantly ask whether their bowl is
			out yet. It was built by people who load kilns, starting in a 14-wheel
			community studio in Portland, not by a generalist software team.
		</p>

		<h2>Built for studios, not investors</h2>
		<p>
			Kilnbook is bootstrapped and profitable. There is no investor pushing us to expand into gyms
			or spas, so the roadmap is set by the working studio owners who use it every day.
			<a href="/about">About Kilnbook</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-alt">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p>
			<h2>Kilnbook and Mindbody side by side</h2>
			<div class="prose">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th scope="col">What your studio needs</th>
							<th scope="col">Kilnbook</th>
							<th scope="col">Mindbody</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Bisque and glaze load queues</th>
							<td>Built in, per kiln</td>
							<td>No concept of kiln loads</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Firing fees by cubic inch</th>
							<td>Calculated and collected automatically</td>
							<td>Manual workarounds</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Firing status and pickup texts</th>
							<td>Automatic</td>
							<td>No equivalent</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Wheel and table reservations by tier</th>
							<td>Built in</td>
							<td>Not equipment-aware</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Waitlists</th>
							<td>Move the next student in automatically</td>
							<td>Managed by staff</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="cta-band">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>Run your studio on studio software.</h2>
			<p>30 days free on the Studio plan. No card to start, and nothing is deleted if you walk away.</p>
			<a href="/contact" class="btn btn-dark">Book a demo</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
